weave-knee trial v1
fanout: 6
seed: 1
weaver: claude-sonnet-5
judge: claude-sonnet-5
quality: 4
narrative:
  Kelnex turns on a single loop that runs through all six modules and closes back on itself. Ostkel, the vault, hands its sealed ledger to Mirtol, the cache; Mirtol streams its evictions to Nexmir, the monitor; Nexmir delegates overflow to Ostnex, the scheduler; Ostnex signals backpressure to Pamost, the ledger; Pamost reports missed heartbeats to Grisax, the relay; and Grisax publishes its sweep verdicts back to Ostkel, completing the circuit.

  Ostkel sits at the head of this cycle as the vault: it keeps only digests of what it carries, never the bodies, and it will not act until two of its peers countersign. When a handoff to Mirtol fails, Ostkel retries at most three times before parking it. What Ostkel eventually hands onward is a sealed ledger, passed whole to Mirtol.

  Mirtol, the cache, receives that sealed ledger and answers reads from its newest snapshot only, refusing any new writes while its own sweep is running. Its handoffs are less forgiving than Ostkel's — a failed handoff to Nexmir is retried at most once before being parked. What Mirtol passes onward is not the ledger itself but its evictions, streamed to Nexmir.

  Nexmir, the monitor, receives that stream of evictions and evicts anything left unclaimed after five ticks. Like Ostkel, it keeps only digests, never bodies, and like Mirtol, it refuses new writes while its sweep is running. What Nexmir passes onward is its overflow, delegated to Ostnex.

  Ostnex, the scheduler, takes that delegated overflow and, like Mirtol, answers reads only from its newest snapshot; like Ostkel, it will not act until two peers countersign. It is the most patient of the six, evicting anything left unclaimed only after twelve ticks. What Ostnex passes onward is a signal of backpressure, sent to Pamost.

  Pamost, the ledger, receives that backpressure signal and, like Ostkel and Nexmir, keeps only digests of what it carries. Like Mirtol and Ostnex, it answers reads from its newest snapshot only, and it evicts anything left unclaimed after seven ticks. What Pamost passes onward is a report of missed heartbeats, sent to Grisax.

  Grisax, the relay, receives that heartbeat report and, like Nexmir, evicts anything left unclaimed after five ticks; like Mirtol, Ostnex, and Pamost, it answers reads from its newest snapshot only. Its retry discipline falls between Ostkel's and Mirtol's: a failed handoff is retried at most twice before being parked. What Grisax passes onward is its sweep verdicts, published back to Ostkel — closing the loop that began there, so the vault's next countersigned action is informed by what the relay has just verdicted.
